-- with its two laws (§5). The grade's fold of §2 is unchanged: the union adds the total `n`.
--
-- Draft 4, 2026-10-17, by Claude Opus 5.5, an agent of the coordinating session, on the brief
-- record/IA/code-follows-2026-10-16/prompt-tables-cut.md (step 3h), after D-2026-10-16-h (item
-- 16: no cutoff accepts a failure; running past a refuted law names it). New: `refutes`,
-- `namedPast`, `standingRefusals` and `assumedPast` (§3); the law `NoCutoffAcceptsAFailure` of
-- `Certifying` (§5). Only the cutoff's parts of §3 and §5 are touched.
--
-- Draft 5, 2026-10-17, by Claude Fable 5.1, an agent of the coordinating session, on the brief
-- record/IA/regions-2026-10-17/prompt-theory.md (step 3j), after D-2026-10-16-i and D-2026-10-16-j
-- (item 15: the evidence of a law kept per binding and per region, the regions from three finite
-- sources, a label informative, the combination the demand's and the weakest by default). New: a
-- region and its sources, the evidence per binding and region, the law's regions from its cutoffs
-- and premises, the grade read over the regions (§2); the theories `Regioning` and `Deriving`
-- beside `Certifying` (§5). `Certifying`'s fields and `Entry` are unchanged.
--
-- What this file states. 6.S16 asks for a theory over a cost type `C`. D-2026-10-14-c says first
-- everything Boolean, so here a law passes or does not, the bound of an entry is `lift` of its
-- outcome (a pass is `0`, a failure `top`, refused by every cutoff, 6.S5), and the evidence cutoff
-- of a demand is the weakest grade it accepts, a parameter of the demand. Where a cost would enter
-- (6.S5, 6.S6, 6.S11, 6.S12, 6.S15) there is a hole `C`, with the comment "D-2026-10-13-m, after
-- the slice's failures". A model of `Certifying` reads the store; `certification_model.lut` is
-- the reference, and the host's certify.py, tester.py and `run`'s refusal are its specimen
-- (6.S16, L14.3). What the brief or the spec leaves open is marked [proposed] at the item above it.
--
-- Index (all sections written in this draft):
--   §1 The entry: its identity, what it holds, its state; the store (7.S9, 7.S12, 7.S13, 6.S6,
--      6.S8, 12.S11).                                                                          done
--   §2 The certificate: the entries of one law, its grade the weakest; transport along an
--      identity and a path, no evidence for free (6.S7, 7.S10, 6.S11, 6.S12, 6.S13).           done
--   §3 What a run requires: `serves`, the refusal, `Certified` (6.S14, 6.S15, 7.S15, 12.S17).  done
--   §4 The hash of a cone (6.S8, 12.S11, 3.S34).                                                 done
--   §5 The theory `Certifying`, the laws of §1 to §3 claimed; the store law `Recording`; the
--      union of the stretches, `Pooling`; the grade over the regions, `Regioning`, and the
--      law's regions, `Deriving`.                                                              done

import "laws.lth" Evidence Coverage Listed Confirmed Supported Cited Assumed NotRun Tested Holds Rates
  Points Drawn spentOf
import "test.lth" Grade Proved ExhaustiveGrade Cases Sampled AssumedGrade gradeRank weakest grade coverageOf
  pointCount
import "generators.lth" Seed Budgets
import "term.lth" Term Pattern Act Program Alpha CanonicalForm Substitution Reduction stepsTo isNormal
  Lam App Pi Sigma Pair Match Inj Sum Data Subtype Hole Record RecordValue Proj Let Declare Define Datum

-- The cost type of 6.S4, and what an entry carries in it (6.S6: the bound observed and the
-- evidence cost), compared with a demand's cutoff in the order of `C` (6.S15) and composed by its
-- `⊕` along an identity or a path (6.S11, 6.S12): holes. D-2026-10-13-m, after the slice's
-- failures. In this draft the bound is `lift` of the outcome (§1) and the evidence is the grade
-- (§2), compared with the grade a demand accepts (§3).
C : U
entryBound : Entry -> C
entryEvidence : Entry -> C

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §1 The entry

Hash = String

-- 7.S9: the binding of an entry, expanded: for each parameter, the generator that drew it, as a
-- functor with its parameters (printed) or as a name, with its fingerprint. A `Binding` of
-- generators.lth (by parameter or by type, in `where` or in a profile) resolved for one parameter
-- (`resolve`) gives one of these.
Drawing = { parameter : String, generator : String, fingerprint : String }

-- A value a run gives a parameter (7.S15: the holes filled by `--set`): a number, or any other
-- value by its printed canonical form. [proposed] Two kinds suffice to compare with a reach: an
-- interval reads numbers, a census compares by equality. Roles: `ReachesAsSets` (§5).
Value : U := data {
  Number : Float64 -> Value ;
  Printed : String -> Value ;
}

-- The reach of a parameter (7.S10, 7.S15): the support of its type, which contains every value
-- the type admits (the type check is the checker's); an interval of numbers, ends included; or the
-- members of a census. Roles: `ReachesAsSets` (§5).
Reach : U := data {
  Support : Reach ;
  Interval : Float64 -> Float64 -> Reach ;
  Members : List Value -> Reach ;
}
ParameterReach = { parameter : String, reach : Reach }

-- What a run that ran gave (6.S3, 7.S12): passed; failed, with its case printed; or not run, with
-- its reason (no generator, no case evaluated with the counts, fuel spent, a hole without a value).
-- A citation (6.R1) and an assumption (6.S10) are entries that passed, with grade proved or assumed
-- [proposed: the outcome is the verdict's, the grade says on what it stands]. Roles:
-- `OnlyAPassServes`, `AFailureRefutes`, `NotRunAddsNothing` (§5).
Outcome : U := data {
  Passed : Outcome ;
  Failed : String -> Outcome ;
  NotRunFor : String -> Outcome ;
  -- [proposed] a sequential test that spent its budget undecided (laws.lth §4): the rates it ran
  -- at and the samples spent. Not a failure and not a pass, so no grade; like `NotRunFor` it adds
  -- nothing to a certificate (`NotRunAddsNothing`).
  Unresolved : Rates -> Outcome ;
}

-- [D-2026-10-15-g] A run cut by its fuel is `NotRunFor fuelSpent` (what case it stopped at and
-- the fuel it was given go in the line it prints, not in the outcome). It is never written to a
-- store (`record`), so the next run of the line runs it again: fuel only cuts a run, and a pass is
-- the same pass at any larger fuel. Recording the fuel a pass used and the fuel an exhaustion was
-- given waits for the costs (D-2026-10-15-g, later).
fuelSpent : String := "fuel spent"
outOfFuel (o : Outcome) : Bool := o == NotRunFor fuelSpent

-- The identity of an outcome: 7.S9's identity of an entry (the law, the hash of its cone, the
-- binding expanded), with the seed and `n` of 7.S13, under which a test is deterministic. The fuel
-- given is not part of it (D-2026-10-15-g), nor of the entry. [the core's bite 4b, 7.S22,
-- D-2026-10-18-f] A law whose body is a conjunction of laws is tested conjunct by conjunct, each
-- conjunct's entry under the law's name and cone: the conjunct (`just "Symmetric Int r"`, as the law
-- applies it) is part of the identity, `nothing` for a law's own entry and for a control's.
Identity = { law : String, cone : Hash, binding : List Drawing, seed : Seed, cases : Nat, conjunct : Maybe String }

-- An entry: its identity; what it holds, the budgets (7.S8), the reach of each parameter (7.S10),
-- the evidence of laws.lth, the grade of test.lth (`nothing` unless it passed), its outcome, the
-- date, the interpreter's revision and the hash of the host code behind each extern the cone
-- calls, by route (6.S8). The counts of 7.S12 and the labels of 7.S14 are in the evidence and its
-- reasons. [proposed] `cases` is the `n` asked, which 7.S12 compares with the cases evaluated.
-- A conjunct's entry (7.S22) names its conjunct and lists the law's conjuncts (`conjuncts`, nil
-- otherwise), so that a conjunct with no entry leaves the law without a grade (tester.py's
-- `_run_conjunction`, certify.py's `certificate_grade_and_empty`; the store omits both fields when
-- the entry is no conjunct's).
Entry = { law : String, cone : Hash, binding : List Drawing, seed : Seed, cases : Nat, conjunct : Maybe String,
          conjuncts : List String,
          budgets : Budgets, reach : List ParameterReach, evidence : Evidence, grade : Maybe Grade,
          outcome : Outcome, date : String, interpreter : String, hosts : List (String * Hash) }

-- The state of a law in the store, as a refusal lists it (12.S11, 12.S17): the outcome of its
-- latest entry at the current hash; stale, when every entry it has is at another hash (the latest
-- such hash kept); uncertified, with no entry; or provisional, a name built on a provisional
-- extern (4.R5), refused whatever its entries. Stale is never stored: it is read against the
-- current hash. [proposed] A passed entry that does not serve (a reach that misses the run's
-- values, a grade below the cutoff) is listed as `Current Passed`; the refusal says it does not
-- serve. Roles: `RefusalSaysWhy`, `ACurrentFailureIsRefused`, `ProvisionalIsRefused` (§5).
-- [D-2026-10-15-f] `Disagrees e f`: an exact replay `e` at the current hash disagreed with the
-- entry `f` of its identity before it, and no later run has agreed with `e`; the law is refused
-- whatever its other entries, naming both. Role: `ADisagreementIsRefused` (§5).
State : U := data {
  Current : Outcome -> State ;
  Stale : Hash -> State ;
  Uncertified : State ;
  Provisional : State ;
  Disagrees : Entry -> Entry -> State ;
}

-- The store: the entries, the latest in front (6.S8). An entry has a grade exactly when it passed
-- (6.S3: only a pass certifies; test.lth: a refutation or nothing settled has no grade). This is
-- the predicate of the type, so every store built owes it: the tester's, and one read from
-- `x.cert.json`. [D-2026-10-15-f] Two entries with the same identity agree (7.S13, determinism: a
-- test is deterministic given its identity), unless something outside the identity changed the
-- result between two runs: then the store keeps both, the replay in front, and that disagreement
-- refuses the law (§3) until a later run agrees with the latest entry (a later agreeing pair) or
-- one of the two is removed from the store by hand (the repository's history keeps it). So
-- `determined` (one outcome per identity: the latest two entries of each identity agree) is no
-- longer the store's predicate: a store where it fails is a store that records a disagreement,
-- which refuses. [proposed] A later agreeing pair settles the disagreement, not a failure among
-- its entries: a failure at the current hash still refutes the law (§2, 6.S3; L10.3: a single
-- counterexample refutes), so a disagreement one side of which failed leaves the law refuted until
-- that failure is removed by hand.
-- [D-2026-10-15-d, L14.4: the hole marked] The store is read here and written by `record` below,
-- but `x.cert.json` (and run.py's `x.results.json`) are read and written by the host, in Python,
-- outside the token `W`: no action of this file touches them, and a store changed by a third party
-- between two reads is not seen by any law. Postponed until both stores become places of the
-- world with actions of their own (10.S6, 12.S11), with the full store of results; until then each
-- run records the hash of the bytes of every store it read (`stores_read`, 12.S18).
-- [proposed, C6 and C11 of the round-1 review] Two more, so that what an entry holds is tied to
-- what it ran (6.S7: the grade is a view of the evidence, not stored apart) and the reader's
-- "latest" is the latest: a pass's grade is one its evidence admits, and a pass over listed cases
-- reaches a census of each parameter, of no more values than the cases it evaluated (6.S7: listed
-- cases give `0` at those cases and `⊤` elsewhere); and no entry is older than one behind it.
-- [proposed, C14 of the round-1 review] Determinism covers the grade too: one identity, one
-- outcome and one grade (the grade is read from the evidence a deterministic run gives, 6.S7).
agree (e f : Entry) : Bool := e.outcome == f.outcome and e.grade == f.grade
-- [D-2026-10-15-f] The disagreements a store records: for each identity, its latest entry and the
-- one of the same identity behind it, when the two disagree. Each function takes the identity of
-- an entry as an argument (a model's `identity`, `k.identity` in the laws), so that a model can
-- read its store with them.
latestWith (ident : Entry -> Identity) (i : Identity) (es : List Entry) : Maybe Entry :=
  match es { nil -> nothing ; cons e rest -> if ident e == i then just e else latestWith ident i rest }
behind (ident : Entry -> Identity) (i : Identity) (es : List Entry) : List Entry :=
  match es { nil -> nil ; cons e rest -> if ident e == i then rest else behind ident i rest }
seenIdentity (i : Identity) (is : List Identity) : Bool := match is { nil -> false ; cons j rest -> i == j or seenIdentity i rest }
disputes (ident : Entry -> Identity) (seen : List Identity) (es : List Entry) : List (Entry * Entry) :=
  match es { nil -> nil ; cons e rest ->
    if seenIdentity (ident e) seen then disputes ident seen rest
    else (match latestWith ident (ident e) rest {
            just f -> if agree e f then disputes ident (cons (ident e) seen) rest
                      else cons (e, f) (disputes ident (cons (ident e) seen) rest) ;
            nothing -> disputes ident (cons (ident e) seen) rest }) }
-- whether the identity's latest two entries disagree
disputedAt (ident : Entry -> Identity) (i : Identity) (es : List Entry) : Bool :=
  match latestWith ident i es { nothing -> false ; just e ->
    (match latestWith ident i (behind ident i es) { nothing -> false ; just f -> not (agree e f) }) }
-- the first disagreement among a law's entries at a hash (the refusal names it)
entriesAt (l : String) (h : Hash) (es : List Entry) : List Entry :=
  match es { nil -> nil ; cons e rest -> if e.law == l and e.cone == h then cons e (entriesAt l h rest) else entriesAt l h rest }
disputeAt (ident : Entry -> Identity) (l : String) (h : Hash) (es : List Entry) : Maybe (Entry * Entry) :=
  match disputes ident nil (entriesAt l h es) { nil -> nothing ; cons p rest -> just p }
-- one outcome per identity: no disagreement recorded
determined (k : Certifying) (es : List Entry) : Bool := match disputes k.identity nil es { nil -> true ; cons p rest -> false }
gradedAsOutcome (es : List Entry) : Bool :=
  match es { nil -> true ; cons e rest -> ((e.outcome == Passed) == not (e.grade == nothing)) and gradedAsOutcome rest }
-- the grades a pass's evidence admits: test.lth's `grade` of a confirming or a supporting verdict
-- over the evidence's coverage (the entry does not hold the verdict), proved for a citation (6.R1),
-- assumed for an assumption (6.S10), none for a run that did not run
admits (ev : Evidence) (g : Grade) : Bool := match ev {
  Cited r -> g == Proved ;
  Assumed -> g == AssumedGrade ;
  NotRun r -> false ;
  -- [proposed] a statistical pass: `StatisticalByTest` (laws.lth §4) settles `Holds` as supported,
  -- over a drawn coverage, which test.lth grades `Sampled`; its rates stay in the evidence, the
  -- price of that grade (D-2026-10-15-b), so a passed entry carries alpha, beta, delta and spent
  Tested v -> (match v { Holds r -> g == Sampled ; _ -> false }) ;
  _ -> grade Confirmed (coverageOf ev) == just g or grade Supported (coverageOf ev) == just g }
lengthOf (vs : List Value) : Nat := match vs { nil -> 0 ; cons v rest -> 1 + lengthOf rest }
censusOfAtMost (n : Nat) (rs : List ParameterReach) : Bool :=
  match rs { nil -> true ; cons r rest ->
    (match r.reach { Members vs -> lengthOf vs <= n ; _ -> false }) and censusOfAtMost n rest }
heldAsRun (es : List Entry) : Bool :=
  match es { nil -> true ; cons e rest ->
    (match e.grade { nothing -> true ; just g ->
       admits e.evidence g
         and (not (coverageOf e.evidence == just Listed) or censusOfAtMost (pointCount e.evidence) e.reach) })
    and heldAsRun rest }
-- the order of two dates as the store writes them (ISO 8601 in UTC, whose order is the order of
-- the text), the first not before the second: a hole, since the kernel orders numbers and not
-- `String`; until it is filled, a store's predicate on two entries or more is not evaluated
notBefore : String -> String -> Bool
latestInFront (es : List Entry) : Bool :=
  match es { nil -> true ; cons e rest ->
    (match rest { nil -> true ; cons f more -> notBefore e.date f.date }) and latestInFront rest }
Store (k : Certifying) : U := { es : List Entry | gradedAsOutcome es and heldAsRun es and latestInFront es }

-- A known outcome is not recomputed (7.S13): the latest entry with the identity asked, whatever
-- its outcome. The tester asks before it runs; `--deep` runs again, and a new seed or `n` is a
-- new identity. A not-run for lack of fuel is never known, since it is never written.
known (k : Certifying) (i : Identity) (es : List Entry) : Maybe Entry := latestWith k.identity i es

-- Recording never edits an entry (6.S8): a new entry goes in front and the old ones stay.
-- [D-2026-10-15-f] Writing a certificate is idempotent (6.S15): an exact replay (an entry whose
-- identity the store has) that agrees with the latest entry of its identity leaves the store as it
-- is, so `--deep` rechecks and adds nothing; one that disagrees goes in front, beside the old, and
-- the law is refused (`Disagrees`, §3); one that agrees with the latest of an identity in
-- disagreement goes in front too, and the latest two then agree, which settles it. A new seed is
-- a new identity and adds an entry. [D-2026-10-15-g] A not-run for lack of fuel is never written.
-- The write itself is the host's, outside `W` (the hole marked at the store above,
-- D-2026-10-15-d). The law of `record` is `Recording` (§5), of which this is the reference.
recordEntry (k : Certifying) (e : Entry) (es : List Entry) : List Entry :=
  if outOfFuel e.outcome then es
  else (match known k (k.identity e) es {
          just f -> if agree e f and not (disputedAt k.identity (k.identity e) es) then es else cons e es ;
          nothing -> cons e es })
record (k : Certifying) (e : Entry) (st : Store k) : Store k := recordEntry k e st

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §2 The certificate

-- A law's certificate (6.S6 for the Boolean case): the law, the current hash of its cone, and its
-- entries. What it is certified on and its grade are read from the entries, not stored apart
-- (6.S7): only the entries of the law at the current hash count (12.S11). [proposed] A failure at
-- the current hash, under any binding, refutes the law, and the certificate has no grade (6.S3: a
-- definition fails when one law fails), and so does a disagreement recorded there (D-2026-10-15-f,
-- `disputed` below); otherwise, under one binding the entries add evidence, so
-- the binding's grade is the strongest of its passes (6.S13: a better grade comes from a new
-- check), and across bindings the grade is the weakest (7.S10: the grade is summarized by the
-- weakest). The cost form of the grade (an evidence cost in `C`, 6.S7) is the hole above.
Certificate = { law : String, cone : Hash, entries : List Entry }
withEntry (c : Certificate) (e : Entry) : Certificate := { law := c.law, cone := c.cone, entries := cons e c.entries }
stronger (g h : Grade) : Grade := if gradeRank g <= gradeRank h then h else g

-- [proposed] The pieces of the fold of §5 (C1 of the round-1 review): entries put in front of a
-- certificate; its entries under one binding, and the others; whether a failure at its hash refutes
-- it, and the latest such failure; the weakest of two grades where `nothing` (no pass yet) is the
-- unit, and the stronger of a grade and a binding's grade so far.
prepend (es : List Entry) (c : Certificate) : Certificate := match es { nil -> c ; cons e rest -> withEntry (prepend rest c) e }
keepUnder (keep : Bool) (b : List Drawing) (es : List Entry) : List Entry :=
  match es { nil -> nil ; cons e rest ->
    if (e.binding == b) == keep then cons e (keepUnder keep b rest) else keepUnder keep b rest }
only (b : List Drawing) (c : Certificate) : Certificate := { law := c.law, cone := c.cone, entries := keepUnder true b c.entries }
without (b : List Drawing) (c : Certificate) : Certificate := { law := c.law, cone := c.cone, entries := keepUnder false b c.entries }
isFailure (o : Outcome) : Bool := match o { Failed x -> true ; _ -> false }
failureAt (l : String) (h : Hash) (es : List Entry) : Maybe Entry :=
  match es { nil -> nothing ; cons e rest -> if e.law == l and e.cone == h and isFailure e.outcome then just e else failureAt l h rest }
refuted (c : Certificate) : Bool := match failureAt c.law c.cone c.entries { just f -> true ; nothing -> false }
-- [D-2026-10-15-f] a disagreement among its entries at its hash leaves a certificate with no
-- grade, as a failure does: the law is refused until the disagreement is resolved
disputed (k : Certifying) (c : Certificate) : Bool :=
  match disputeAt k.identity c.law c.cone c.entries { just p -> true ; nothing -> false }
standsOnNothing (k : Certifying) (c : Certificate) : Bool := refuted c or disputed k c
orWeakest (m n : Maybe Grade) : Maybe Grade :=
  match m { nothing -> n ; just g -> (match n { nothing -> m ; just h -> just (weakest g h) }) }
orStronger (g : Grade) (m : Maybe Grade) : Grade := match m { nothing -> g ; just h -> stronger g h }
-- an entry moved under a binding, and a list of them (how the fold's laws put passes under one)
under (b : List Drawing) (e : Entry) : Entry :=
  { law := e.law, cone := e.cone, binding := b, seed := e.seed, cases := e.cases, conjunct := e.conjunct,
    conjuncts := e.conjuncts, budgets := e.budgets, reach := e.reach,
    evidence := e.evidence, grade := e.grade, outcome := e.outcome, date := e.date, interpreter := e.interpreter, hosts := e.hosts }
underAll (b : List Drawing) (es : List Entry) : List Entry := match es { nil -> nil ; cons e rest -> cons (under b e) (underAll b rest) }

-- [D-2026-10-16-g] The union of a law's stretches. A stretch is one run of a law under one binding
-- and one seed; its entry keeps its seed and its `n`. The seed names a sample of cases, and the
-- samples of one law, cone and binding (one distribution) are one sample of their total size
-- (L10.3: sampling a deterministic law bounds the fraction of cases where it fails, with `n` the
-- total; one counterexample still refutes it). So the grade of a law under a binding is read on
-- the union of its agreeing stretches: for an exact law, the sum of their cases with no failure
-- among them (a failure refutes, above); for a statistical law, the sequential test continued
-- across them (laws.lth §4, `ratioAfter`: each stretch starts from the ratio the earlier ones
-- left), its samples summed. A replay of a stretch, its identity again, is not a new stretch: the
-- store absorbs it when it agrees and keeps it beside when it disagrees (§1, `Recording`), and a
-- disagreement in any stretch refuses the union (`disputed`). The stretches in the union are the
-- passes at the current hash under the binding and, for a statistical law, the stretches it left
-- unresolved, which a later one continued. [proposed] A stretch's cases are a sample of its seed
-- only when they were drawn (a statistical test, or points over `Drawn`): every value of a type, or
-- a census, gives the same cases whatever the seed, so all its seeds are one stretch (the key
-- `nothing`). [proposed] Of two entries of one seed under one binding the larger `n` counts: a run
-- of n cases from a seed is the first n of any larger run of it (7.S1: the seed threaded draw by
-- draw). The grade is the fold above, unchanged; the union adds its `n`, whose cost form
-- (ε̂(n, δ), 7.S11) is the hole above. The law is `Pooling` (§5).
-- [D-2026-10-16-g, the budget the whole test's] A statistical stretch's size is the samples it
-- drew itself (the specimen writes the rates' `spent` less the samples before it). Its budget is
-- the demand's for the whole test, not part of the distribution, so the specimen reads the binding
-- of a statistical stretch without the drawing of its cutoffs (the parameter of type `Sequential`,
-- which its evidence names): stretches run under two budgets are one union. Here `Entry` does not
-- name that parameter, so `pooledCases` compares bindings whole; stating it waits for the entry to
-- carry its cutoffs' parameter (a gap of this draft, the specimen ahead of the reference).
drawnStretch (e : Entry) : Bool :=
  match e.evidence { Tested v -> true ; Points A pts c -> (match c { Drawn s -> true ; _ -> false }) ; _ -> false }
stretchKey (e : Entry) : Maybe Seed := if drawnStretch e then just e.seed else nothing
stretchSize (e : Entry) : Nat := match e.evidence { Tested v -> spentOf v ; _ -> e.cases }
inTheUnion (e : Entry) : Bool := match e.outcome { Passed -> true ; Unresolved r -> true ; _ -> false }
-- the stretches of a law at a hash under a binding, each by its key and its size
stretchesUnder (l : String) (h : Hash) (b : List Drawing) (es : List Entry) : List (Maybe Seed * Nat) :=
  match es { nil -> nil ; cons e rest ->
    if e.law == l and e.cone == h and e.binding == b and inTheUnion e
    then cons (stretchKey e, stretchSize e) (stretchesUnder l h b rest) else stretchesUnder l h b rest }
hasKey (k : Maybe Seed) (ps : List (Maybe Seed * Nat)) : Bool := match ps { nil -> false ; cons (j, n) rest -> j == k or hasKey k rest }
largestAt (k : Maybe Seed) (ps : List (Maybe Seed * Nat)) (m : Nat) : Nat :=
  match ps { nil -> m ; cons (j, n) rest -> largestAt k rest (if j == k and m < n then n else m) }
seenKey (k : Maybe Seed) (ks : List (Maybe Seed)) : Bool := match ks { nil -> false ; cons j rest -> j == k or seenKey k rest }
sumOfLargest (seen : List (Maybe Seed)) (all ps : List (Maybe Seed * Nat)) : Nat :=
  match ps { nil -> 0 ; cons (k, n) rest ->
    if seenKey k seen then sumOfLargest seen all rest else largestAt k all 0 + sumOfLargest (cons k seen) all rest }
-- the size of the union: each key once, at its largest
pooledCases (c : Certificate) (b : List Drawing) : Nat :=
  sumOfLargest nil (stretchesUnder c.law c.cone b c.entries) (stretchesUnder c.law c.cone b c.entries)
-- one grade at most another, where `nothing` (no grade) is below every grade
atMost (m n : Maybe Grade) : Bool :=
  match m { nothing -> true ; just g -> (match n { nothing -> false ; just h -> gradeRank g <= gradeRank h }) }

-- [D-2026-10-16-i, D-2026-10-16-j] The evidence of a law is kept per binding and per region, not
-- summarized: each region with its `n` and its bound. A region is named by its source and its
-- name, and the sources are three and finite, so the regions of a law are bounded by the size of
-- the theory, the law and the code: the type, whose theory declares its degenerate cases once
-- (generators.lth §9, `Degeneracies`; arrays.lth §5); the law, each cutoff parameter giving the
-- side just above it and the side just below, each premise its boundary (`lawRegions` below); and
-- the code, each branch of the cone (an `if`, a `match`: branch coverage), which the tester reads
-- from the cone and names. A label an author adds (`κ` above 1e8, 7.S14) is a fourth source,
-- recorded with its share of cases and informative (L10.11): it enters no grade and no combination
-- (`grades`). The bound of a region is ε̂(n, δ) (L10.3, 7.S11), the cost form, a hole as
-- `entryBound` is; in this draft a region with cases has the bound of its certificate (a pass `0`,
-- a failure anywhere `top`) and a region with none bounds nothing, which caps the grade
-- (`regionGrade`). The evidence per region is the entries' (7.S9: an entry records its labels);
-- here it is given beside the certificate, as `BindingEvidence`, since `Entry` does not carry it
-- yet [proposed: the labels' field of 7.S9, a gap of this draft as the cutoffs' parameter is].
Source : U := data { FromType : Source ; FromLaw : Source ; FromCode : Source ; Label : Source }
Region = { source : Source, name : String }
grades (r : Region) : Bool := match r.source { Label -> false ; _ -> true }
RegionEvidence = { region : Region, n : Nat }
regionBound : RegionEvidence -> C
BindingEvidence = { binding : List Drawing, regions : List RegionEvidence }
-- the cases of a region recorded under a binding, none when none is recorded
casesIn (r : Region) (rs : List RegionEvidence) : Nat :=
  match rs { nil -> 0 ; cons e rest -> (if e.region == r then e.n else 0) + casesIn r rest }
casesAt (b : List Drawing) (r : Region) (ev : List BindingEvidence) : Nat :=
  match ev { nil -> 0 ; cons x rest -> (if x.binding == b then casesIn r x.regions else 0) + casesAt b r rest }
-- the evidence with a region's cases under a binding added, or removed (how the laws of §5 build
-- the evidence they quantify over)
withCases (b : List Drawing) (r : Region) (n : Nat) (ev : List BindingEvidence) : List BindingEvidence :=
  cons { binding := b, regions := [{ region := r, n := n }] } ev
dropIn (r : Region) (rs : List RegionEvidence) : List RegionEvidence :=
  match rs { nil -> nil ; cons e rest -> if e.region == r then dropIn r rest else cons e (dropIn r rest) }
dropCases (b : List Drawing) (r : Region) (ev : List BindingEvidence) : List BindingEvidence :=
  match ev { nil -> nil ; cons x rest ->
    cons (if x.binding == b then { binding := x.binding, regions := dropIn r x.regions } else x) (dropCases b r rest) }
-- The law's regions, the second source: each cutoff parameter its two sides, each premise its
-- boundary, named by the parameter as the law writes it. Which parameters are cutoffs
-- (`Tolerance`, `RankFloor`, `Sequential`: the types the demand supplies, D-2026-10-14-c,
-- D-2026-10-16-k item 20) and which are premises (a parameter of a subtype, `s : PsdAt m fl`) the
-- tester reads from the law's signature. [proposed] The boundary of a premise is where its
-- predicate barely holds: at the cutoff for a predicate stated by one (`PsdAt m fl`, barely
-- semidefinite at the floor); for an exact predicate (`Symmetric`) the boundary is the premise
-- itself, and the region records that the premise held (7.S14). The law is `Deriving` (§5).
above (c : String) : Region := { source := FromLaw, name := c <> " above" }
below (c : String) : Region := { source := FromLaw, name := c <> " below" }
boundary (p : String) : Region := { source := FromLaw, name := p <> " boundary" }
cutoffRegions (cs : List String) : List Region :=
  match cs { nil -> nil ; cons c rest -> cons (above c) (cons (below c) (cutoffRegions rest)) }
premiseRegions (ps : List String) : List Region := match ps { nil -> nil ; cons p rest -> cons (boundary p) (premiseRegions rest) }
appendRegions (xs ys : List Region) : List Region := match xs { nil -> ys ; cons x rest -> cons x (appendRegions rest ys) }
lawRegions (cutoffs premises : List String) : List Region := appendRegions (cutoffRegions cutoffs) (premiseRegions premises)
memberRegion (r : Region) (rs : List Region) : Bool := match rs { nil -> false ; cons s rest -> s == r or memberRegion r rest }
allIn (rs within : List Region) : Bool := match rs { nil -> true ; cons r rest -> memberRegion r within and allIn rest within }
countRegions (rs : List Region) : Nat := match rs { nil -> 0 ; cons r rest -> 1 + countRegions rest }
countStrings (xs : List String) : Nat := match xs { nil -> 0 ; cons x rest -> 1 + countStrings rest }
-- The grade of a law in one region under one binding: none where the certificate stands on
-- nothing; the binding's grade (the fold above) where the region has cases under it; and at most
-- sampled where it has none (D-2026-10-16-i: a sample says nothing of a null set, so the law is
-- below cases there, and a census or an enumeration that reached no case of the region does not
-- say of it what its grade says). The grade over the regions is their combination, region by
-- region and then binding by binding, with the combination `comb` the demand chooses (5.S8), the
-- weakest by default (`orWeakest`, the reference model `regioning`): with no region it is the
-- certificate's grade, a label is passed over, and a certificate with no pass has none. The law is
-- `Regioning` (§5).
capped (m : Maybe Grade) : Maybe Grade := match m { nothing -> nothing ; just g -> just (weakest g Sampled) }
orStrongest (m n : Maybe Grade) : Maybe Grade :=
  match m { nothing -> n ; just g -> (match n { nothing -> m ; just h -> just (stronger g h) }) }
regionGrade (k : Certifying) (c : Certificate) (b : List Drawing) (r : Region) (ev : List BindingEvidence) : Maybe Grade :=
  if standsOnNothing k c then nothing
  else if casesAt b r ev == 0 then capped (k.grade (only b c)) else k.grade (only b c)
overRegions (k : Certifying) (comb : Maybe Grade -> Maybe Grade -> Maybe Grade) (c : Certificate) (b : List Drawing)
            (rs : List Region) (ev : List BindingEvidence) : Maybe Grade :=
  match rs { nil -> k.grade (only b c) ; cons r rest ->
    if grades r then comb (regionGrade k c b r ev) (overRegions k comb c b rest ev) else overRegions k comb c b rest ev }
-- the bindings of the passes that count, each once, in the order of the entries
bindingSeen (b : List Drawing) (bs : List (List Drawing)) : Bool := match bs { nil -> false ; cons x rest -> x == b or bindingSeen b rest }
bindingsFrom (c : Certificate) (es : List Entry) (seen : List (List Drawing)) : List (List Drawing) :=
  match es { nil -> nil ; cons e rest ->
    if e.law == c.law and e.cone == c.cone and e.outcome == Passed and not (e.grade == nothing) and not (bindingSeen e.binding seen)
    then cons e.binding (bindingsFrom c rest (cons e.binding seen)) else bindingsFrom c rest seen }
bindingsOf (c : Certificate) : List (List Drawing) := bindingsFrom c c.entries nil
overBindings (k : Certifying) (comb : Maybe Grade -> Maybe Grade -> Maybe Grade) (c : Certificate) (bs : List (List Drawing))
             (rs : List Region) (ev : List BindingEvidence) : Maybe Grade :=
  match bs { nil -> nothing ; cons b rest -> comb (overRegions k comb c b rs ev) (overBindings k comb c rest rs ev) }
regionedGrade (k : Certifying) (comb : Maybe Grade -> Maybe Grade -> Maybe Grade) (c : Certificate) (rs : List Region)
              (ev : List BindingEvidence) : Maybe Grade :=
  if standsOnNothing k c then nothing else overBindings k comb c (bindingsOf c) rs ev

-- The older names of a grade (point, census of k, bounded, witnessed, sampled) read as the five
-- (6.S7, decided D-2026-10-13-g; 7.S10): a point is cases with one case, a census is cases,
-- bounded is exhaustive on the box its domain names, witnessed is cases with a witness.
gradeOfOldName (s : String) : Maybe Grade :=
  if s == "point" then just Cases else if s == "census" then just Cases
  else if s == "bounded" then just ExhaustiveGrade else if s == "witnessed" then just Cases
  else if s == "sampled" then just Sampled else if s == "proved" then just Proved
  else if s == "assumed" then just AssumedGrade else if s == "exhaustive" then just ExhaustiveGrade
  else if s == "cases" then just Cases else nothing

-- What a law stands certified on, and with which grade: the reach of each parameter (7.S10) and the
-- grade. Transport (6.S11) takes B's standing and the standing of the identity of A and B, and gives
-- A on the intersection of the two, with the weaker grade; on nothing outside it. For the Boolean
-- identity of this draft the distance is `lift true = 0`, so B's certificate transports exactly;
-- an identity valued in `C` adds its distance by `⊕` (the hole above). [proposed] A parameter that
-- only one of the two standings reaches is not reached by the transport.
Standing = { law : String, on : List ParameterReach, grade : Grade }
-- [proposed, C7 of the round-1 review] The identity transport goes along is a link: the name whose
-- certificate is carried (`from`, B), the name it is carried to (`to`, A), and the standing of the
-- law A ≡ B. Transport takes a link only from the law of the standing it carries and gives
-- nothing otherwise: before, any standing passed as "the identity" carried B's certificate to any
-- name. That two sampled links share a distribution (6.S12, L10.8) is a cost, the hole above.
Link = { from : String, to : String, standing : Standing }

-- Along a path (6.S12): link by link, each link the standing of an identity and the name it reaches.
-- Two links sampled over one distribution compose by the union bound, their failure fractions and
-- their δ summed: that is a cost, the hole above (D-2026-10-13-m, after the slice's failures); here
-- the grades meet by `weakest` and the reaches intersect.
-- [proposed, C7] Each step names the next name and the standing of its identity with the last, so
-- the link starts where the path stands; a link the model refuses ends the path with nothing.
linkFrom (b : Standing) (a : String) (i : Standing) : Link := { from := b.law, to := a, standing := i }
along (k : Certifying) (b : Standing) (path : List (String * Standing)) : Maybe Standing :=
  match path { nil -> just b ; cons (a, i) rest ->
    (match k.transport b (linkFrom b a i) { nothing -> nothing ; just s -> along k s rest }) }

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §3 What a run requires

-- A demand on one law (6.S15, 7.S15): the law, the current hash of its cone, what the run gives
-- its parameters (the holes filled by `--set`, compared when the run starts), and the cutoff, the
-- weakest grade the demand accepts (D-2026-10-14-c: a parameter the demand supplies). An assumed
-- entry serves only a demand that accepts `AssumedGrade` (6.S10: the demand names the price of
-- assumptions, with no default). [proposed] The run gives each law its values by parameter name;
-- values computed during the run are compared at each call under `--checked` (7.S15), not here.
-- The cost cutoff of 6.S15 (a bound and an evidence cost in `C`) is the hole above.
Demand = { law : String, cone : Hash, given : List (String * Value), accepts : Grade }
withAccepts (d : Demand) (g : Grade) : Demand := { law := d.law, cone := d.cone, given := d.given, accepts := g }

-- the reach recorded for a parameter, and whether the reaches cover what the run gives
-- [proposed: a value given to a parameter the entry records no reach for is not covered]
reachOf (p : String) (rs : List ParameterReach) : Maybe Reach :=
  match rs { nil -> nothing ; cons r rest -> if r.parameter == p then just r.reach else reachOf p rest }
coversAll (contains : Reach -> Value -> Bool) (rs : List ParameterReach) (given : List (String * Value)) : Bool :=
  match given { nil -> true ; cons (p, v) rest ->
    (match reachOf p rs { just r -> contains r v ; nothing -> false }) and coversAll contains rs rest }
anyServes (serves : Demand -> Entry -> Bool) (d : Demand) (es : List Entry) : Bool :=
  match es { nil -> false ; cons e rest -> serves d e or anyServes serves d rest }
-- [proposed, C2 of the round-1 review] An entry of a demand's law with the reach, outcome and
-- grade given, the rest as drawn; and the reach that covers whatever a demand gives, the support of
-- each parameter it names, in front of a reach drawn. The laws of §5 draw the entries that would
-- serve a demand but for one condition as this image (7.S6: a subtype a filter reaches too rarely
-- is the image of a construction whose arguments are drawn).
reshape (e : Entry) (l : String) (r : List ParameterReach) (o : Outcome) (g : Maybe Grade) : Entry :=
  { law := l, cone := e.cone, binding := e.binding, seed := e.seed, cases := e.cases, conjunct := e.conjunct,
    conjuncts := e.conjuncts, budgets := e.budgets, reach := r,
    evidence := e.evidence, grade := g, outcome := o, date := e.date, interpreter := e.interpreter, hosts := e.hosts }
withSupport (given : List (String * Value)) (rs : List ParameterReach) : List ParameterReach :=
  match given { nil -> rs ; cons (p, v) rest -> cons { parameter := p, reach := Support } (withSupport rest rs) }
toward (d : Demand) (e : Entry) (o : Outcome) (g : Maybe Grade) : Entry := reshape e d.law (withSupport d.given e.reach) o g

-- The dependency cone of the entry of a run (6.S14, 12.S17): each name, whether it is provisional
-- (4.R5), and the demands on its laws. A name whose type states no law is certified with nothing
-- to run [proposed, 6.S1: certified when each law its type states has a passing entry].
ConeName = { name : String, provisional : Bool, laws : List Demand }
Refusal = { name : String, law : Maybe String, state : State }
appendRefusals (xs ys : List Refusal) : List Refusal := match xs { nil -> ys ; cons x rest -> cons x (appendRefusals rest ys) }
-- the latest entry of a law, and the latest at a hash
latestOf (l : String) (es : List Entry) : Maybe Entry :=
  match es { nil -> nothing ; cons e rest -> if e.law == l then just e else latestOf l rest }
latestAt (l : String) (h : Hash) (es : List Entry) : Maybe Entry :=
  match es { nil -> nothing ; cons e rest -> if e.law == l and e.cone == h then just e else latestAt l h rest }
appendEntries (xs ys : List Entry) : List Entry := match xs { nil -> ys ; cons x rest -> cons x (appendEntries rest ys) }

-- What a run requires (7.S15, 12.S17, 6.S14): a cone the store refuses nothing of. `run` starts
-- only on a value of this type, and otherwise lists each refusal with its state; there is no flag
-- that opts out (D-2026-10-12-y). A cone one of whose laws has a failure at the current hash is
-- never of this type, whatever entry serves it (`ACurrentFailureIsRefused`), nor one of whose laws
-- has a disagreement recorded there (`ADisagreementIsRefused`, D-2026-10-15-f). `test` demands
-- certificates without values (6.S14), which this type does not cover.
Certified (k : Certifying) (es : List Entry) : U := { cone : List ConeName | k.refuse es cone == nil }

-- [D-2026-10-16-h, item 16] No cutoff accepts a failure. A failure's bound is `top` (6.S5), which no
-- cutoff admits: whatever grade a demand accepts, the weakest (`AssumedGrade`) included, an entry
-- that failed serves nothing (`NoCutoffAcceptsAFailure`, §5), and a law with a failure or a
-- disagreement at the current hash is refused whatever serves (`ACurrentFailureIsRefused`,
-- `ADisagreementIsRefused`). Lowering the cutoff is never the way past a refuted law; naming it is.
-- The run's demand holds, beside its cutoff, the names it runs as assumed (demand.lth's `assumes`,
-- `run --accept assumed:NAME`): a provisional extern (D-2026-10-16-c, 10.S10) or a refuted law. A
-- refusal of a refuted law whose name the demand holds is lifted, and the law enters the run's
-- record with grade assumed and the names of the cone downstream of the name that states it, every
-- run that names it warning on standard error (L5.20). A refusal nobody named stands, so a
-- refutation forgotten deep in the cone still refuses the run, and a refusal for another state
-- (stale, uncertified, provisional) is not lifted by naming the law. run.py's `certification` is
-- the specimen. [proposed] The law is named by its name alone, so naming it lifts its refusal on
-- every name of the cone whose theory states it.
refutes (s : State) : Bool := match s { Current o -> isFailure o ; Disagrees e f -> true ; _ -> false }
nameIn (x : String) (xs : List String) : Bool := match xs { nil -> false ; cons y rest -> x == y or nameIn x rest }
namedPast (assumes : List String) (r : Refusal) : Bool :=
  match r.law { nothing -> false ; just l -> nameIn l assumes and refutes r.state }
-- the refusals a run still owes after the names it assumes, and the ones it runs past as assumed
standingRefusals (assumes : List String) (rs : List Refusal) : List Refusal :=
  match rs { nil -> nil ; cons r rest -> if namedPast assumes r then standingRefusals assumes rest else cons r (standingRefusals assumes rest) }
assumedPast (assumes : List String) (rs : List Refusal) : List Refusal :=
  match rs { nil -> nil ; cons r rest -> if namedPast assumes r then cons r (assumedPast assumes rest) else assumedPast assumes rest }

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §4 The hash of a cone

-- What the hash of a cone covers (6.S8, 12.S11): the code of the cone, each name with its
-- definition (a hole filled is a definition, so an entry is kept per filling); the hash of the host
-- code behind each extern it calls, by route (the source files of the module's directory, or the
-- installed package and its version); and the interpreter's revision. [proposed] The code and the
-- hosts are listed in one order the host fixes (by name, by route), so a list is a cone.
Cone = { code : List (String * Term), hosts : List (String * Hash), interpreter : String }
-- the code in canonical form (3.S34): names without module prefix, terms canonical, the binders'
-- annotations erased (D-2026-10-15-e, below)
canonCode (a : Alpha) (m : CanonicalForm a) (code : List (String * Term)) : List (String * Term) :=
  match code { nil -> nil ; cons (x, t) rest -> cons (m.unqualify x, m.canon (eraseBinders t)) (canonCode a m rest) }

-- [D-2026-10-15-e] The hash erases the type annotation of each `Lam` and each `Let`, which the
-- elaborator writes (from a parameter's written type, from the expected type, inferred for a
-- block's `Let`): the hash is the strict level of equality, fixed by the language, and an
-- annotation that leaves the term's behaviour unchanged must not stale a certificate. A type's `Pi`,
-- `Sigma`, `Subtype` and record fields are kept: they are types, which the tester draws from. The
-- condition is the law `ErasureKeepsTheStep`: one step of a term in a program, erased, agrees up to
-- alpha with one step of the erased term in the erased program, so by induction the two reduce
-- alike at any fuel. The reference is `eraseBinders`; the host's is certify.py's `erase`, checked
-- against kernel.py on the library's code in tests/test_certify.py.
erased : Term := Hole "erased"
eraseBinders (t : Term) : Term := match t {
  Lam x a b -> Lam x erased (eraseBinders b) ;
  Let x a v b -> Let x erased (eraseBinders v) (eraseBinders b) ;
  App f a -> App (eraseBinders f) (eraseBinders a) ;
  Pi x a b -> Pi x (eraseBinders a) (eraseBinders b) ;
  Sigma x a b -> Sigma x (eraseBinders a) (eraseBinders b) ;
  Subtype x a b -> Subtype x (eraseBinders a) (eraseBinders b) ;
  Pair a b -> Pair (eraseBinders a) (eraseBinders b) ;
  Inj l a -> Inj l (eraseBinders a) ;
  Match v arms -> Match (eraseBinders v) (eraseArms arms) ;
  Sum kvs -> Sum (erasePairs kvs) ;
  Data n cs -> Data n (erasePairs cs) ;
  Record kvs -> Record (erasePairs kvs) ;
  RecordValue kvs -> RecordValue (erasePairs kvs) ;
  Proj r l -> Proj (eraseBinders r) l ;
  _ -> t ;
}
eraseArms (arms : List (Pattern * Term)) : List (Pattern * Term) := match arms { nil -> nil ;
  cons (p, b) rest -> cons (p, eraseBinders b) (eraseArms rest) }
erasePairs (kvs : List (String * Term)) : List (String * Term) :=
  match kvs { nil -> nil ; cons (k, v) rest -> cons (k, eraseBinders v) (erasePairs rest) }

Erasure (a : Alpha) (sb : Substitution a) (m : Reduction a sb) : { erase : Term -> Term }
-- a program with each definition's type and body erased, and each declaration's type
eraseProgram (f : Term -> Term) (p : Program) : Program := match p { nil -> nil ;
  cons act rest -> cons (match act { Define x ty b -> Define x (f ty) (f b) ; Declare x ty -> Declare x (f ty) ;
                                     Datum v -> Datum v }) (eraseProgram f rest) }
ErasureKeepsTheStep (a : Alpha) (sb : Substitution a) (m : Reduction a sb) (e : Erasure a sb m) (p : Program) (t : Term) : Bool :=
  match m.step p t {
    nothing -> isNormal (m.step (eraseProgram e.erase p) (e.erase t)) ;
    just u -> stepsTo a (m.step (eraseProgram e.erase p) (e.erase t)) (e.erase u) }
Erasure (a : Alpha) (sb : Substitution a) (m : Reduction a sb) := ErasureKeepsTheStep a sb m

-- The hash is the host's (an extern, certification_model.lut). Its laws (6.S8): it is a function
-- of the canonical form, and a change to the code, to the host code or to the interpreter changes
-- it. [proposed] "Changes it" is stated as: two cones that differ there have different hashes; a
-- collision of the hash function is the host's, and a sample that finds one refutes the model.
ConeHashing (a : Alpha) (m : CanonicalForm a) : { hash : Cone -> Hash }
HashOfTheCanonicalForm (a : Alpha) (m : CanonicalForm a) (h : ConeHashing a m) (c : Cone) : Bool :=
  h.hash { code := canonCode a m c.code, hosts := c.hosts, interpreter := c.interpreter } == h.hash c
HashSeesTheCode (a : Alpha) (m : CanonicalForm a) (h : ConeHashing a m) (c : Cone) (code : List (String * Term)) : Bool :=
  canonCode a m code == canonCode a m c.code
    or not (h.hash { code := code, hosts := c.hosts, interpreter := c.interpreter } == h.hash c)
HashSeesTheHost (a : Alpha) (m : CanonicalForm a) (h : ConeHashing a m) (c : Cone) (hosts : List (String * Hash)) : Bool :=
  hosts == c.hosts or not (h.hash { code := c.code, hosts := hosts, interpreter := c.interpreter } == h.hash c)
HashSeesTheInterpreter (a : Alpha) (m : CanonicalForm a) (h : ConeHashing a m) (c : Cone) (r : String) : Bool :=
  r == c.interpreter or not (h.hash { code := c.code, hosts := c.hosts, interpreter := r } == h.hash c)
ConeHashing (a : Alpha) (m : CanonicalForm a) := HashOfTheCanonicalForm a m & HashSeesTheCode a m
  & HashSeesTheHost a m & HashSeesTheInterpreter a m

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §5 The theory

-- A model of certification reads the store: the identity of an entry, whether it is stale against
-- a hash, the grade of a certificate, whether a reach contains a value, whether an entry serves a
-- demand, what a cone is refused, and transport along an identity.
Certifying : {
  identity : Entry -> Identity,
  stale : Hash -> Entry -> Bool,
  grade : Certificate -> Maybe Grade,
  contains : Reach -> Value -> Bool,
  serves : Demand -> Entry -> Bool,
  refuse : List Entry -> List ConeName -> List Refusal,
  transport : Standing -> Link -> Maybe Standing,
}

-- §1. 7.S9, 7.S13: the identity of an outcome is the law, the cone's hash, the binding expanded,
-- the seed and `n`, and the conjunct of a conjunction's entry (7.S22), and nothing the entry holds
-- besides
IdentityIsTheKey (k : Certifying) (e : Entry) : Bool :=
  k.identity e == { law := e.law, cone := e.cone, binding := e.binding, seed := e.seed, cases := e.cases,
                    conjunct := e.conjunct }
-- 12.S11: an entry is stale when its hash differs from the current hash of its cone
StaleIsAnotherHash (k : Certifying) (h : Hash) (e : Entry) : Bool := k.stale h e == not (e.cone == h)

-- [proposed, C2 of the round-1 review] The domains of the laws below. A premise such as
-- `e.law == c.law and e.cone == c.cone`, over an `e` and a `c` drawn apart, holds only when two
-- random strings coincide, so as sampled the law was tested almost nowhere (7.S12 counts such a case
-- apart; L9.6: the grade of a sampled certificate depends on the distribution sampled). Here the
-- premise is the domain: an entry of a certificate's law at its hash, one that passed there with
-- a grade, one that failed there; an entry of a law at any hash (a drawn hash: stale); an entry of
-- a demand's law at its hash, and one that failed there. The tester builds a field a refinement
-- equates to a value and filters the rest (7.S6).
EntryOf (c : Certificate) : U := { e : Entry | e.law == c.law and e.cone == c.cone }
CurrentPass (c : Certificate) : U :=
  { e : Entry | e.law == c.law and e.cone == c.cone and e.outcome == Passed and not (e.grade == nothing) }
CurrentFailure (c : Certificate) : U := { e : Entry | e.law == c.law and e.cone == c.cone and isFailure e.outcome }
EntryOfLaw (l : String) : U := { e : Entry | e.law == l }
EntryFor (d : Demand) : U := { e : Entry | e.law == d.law and e.cone == d.cone }
FailureFor (d : Demand) : U := { e : Entry | e.law == d.law and e.cone == d.cone and isFailure e.outcome }
-- a list of these read as a list of entries (the inclusion, element by element)
passEntries (c : Certificate) (ps : List (CurrentPass c)) : List Entry := match ps { nil -> nil ; cons e rest -> cons e (passEntries c rest) }
withPasses (c : Certificate) (ps : List (CurrentPass c)) : Certificate := prepend (passEntries c ps) c
entriesFor (d : Demand) (xs : List (EntryFor d)) : List Entry := match xs { nil -> nil ; cons e rest -> cons e (entriesFor d rest) }
entriesOfLaw (l : String) (ys : List (EntryOfLaw l)) : List Entry := match ys { nil -> nil ; cons e rest -> cons e (entriesOfLaw l rest) }

-- §2. 6.S7, 7.S10: a certificate with no entry has no grade; a failure at the current hash
-- refutes, wherever it stands among the entries
NoEntryNoGrade (k : Certifying) (l : String) (h : Hash) : Bool := k.grade { law := l, cone := h, entries := nil } == nothing
AFailureRefutes (k : Certifying) (c : Certificate) (f : CurrentFailure c) (ps : List (CurrentPass c)) (es : List Entry) : Bool :=
  k.grade (prepend es (prepend (passEntries c ps) (withEntry c f))) == nothing
-- 12.S11, 7.S12, 6.S13: an entry of another law or a stale one adds nothing, nor an entry of the
-- law that neither failed nor passed with a grade (not run; a pass with no grade `Store` excludes),
-- whatever passes the certificate holds
OnlyCurrentEntriesCount (k : Certifying) (c : Certificate) (ps : List (CurrentPass c)) (e : Entry) (s : EntryOfLaw c.law) : Bool :=
  ((e.law == c.law and not (k.stale c.cone e)) or k.grade (withEntry (withPasses c ps) e) == k.grade (withPasses c ps))
    and (not (k.stale c.cone s) or k.grade (withEntry (withPasses c ps) s) == k.grade (withPasses c ps))
-- [D-2026-10-15-f] unless it is a replay that starts a disagreement (it disagrees with the latest
-- entry of its identity) or settles one (it agrees with the latest of an identity in disagreement)
NotRunAddsNothing (k : Certifying) (c : Certificate) (ps : List (CurrentPass c)) (e : EntryOf c) : Bool :=
  startsOrSettles k (withPasses c ps) e
    or (match e.outcome {
          Failed x -> true ;
          Passed -> not (e.grade == nothing) or k.grade (withEntry (withPasses c ps) e) == k.grade (withPasses c ps) ;
          NotRunFor r -> k.grade (withEntry (withPasses c ps) e) == k.grade (withPasses c ps) ;
          Unresolved r -> k.grade (withEntry (withPasses c ps) e) == k.grade (withPasses c ps) })
startsOrSettles (k : Certifying) (c : Certificate) (e : Entry) : Bool :=
  disputedAt k.identity (k.identity e) c.entries or disputedAt k.identity (k.identity e) (cons e c.entries)
-- 7.S10 [proposed reading of §2], as one step of the fold, so that with the laws above the grade of
-- every certificate is fixed, by induction on its entries (C1 of the round-1 review: the earlier
-- pair fixed it only for two passes added to a certificate with no entry). A pass `p` at the
-- current hash, added where no failure and no disagreement is (D-2026-10-15-f): under its binding, the stronger of its grade and that
-- binding's grade so far; overall, the weakest of that and the grade of the other bindings. The
-- certificate is drawn with passes put under `p`'s binding (`ps`) and passes under bindings drawn
-- (`qs`) in front of it, so that both cases are met; with `ps` and `qs` empty it is any certificate.
pool (c : Certificate) (p : CurrentPass c) (ps qs : List (CurrentPass c)) : Certificate :=
  prepend (passEntries c qs) (prepend (underAll p.binding (passEntries c ps)) c)
StrongestUnderOneBinding (k : Certifying) (c : Certificate) (ps : List (CurrentPass c)) (p : CurrentPass c) : Bool :=
  standsOnNothing k (withEntry (pool c p ps nil) p)
    or (match p.grade { nothing -> true ; just g ->
          k.grade (withEntry (only p.binding (pool c p ps nil)) p)
            == just (orStronger g (k.grade (only p.binding (pool c p ps nil)))) })
WeakestAcrossBindings (k : Certifying) (c : Certificate) (ps qs : List (CurrentPass c)) (p : CurrentPass c) : Bool :=
  standsOnNothing k (withEntry (pool c p ps qs) p)
    or k.grade (withEntry (pool c p ps qs) p)
         == orWeakest (k.grade (without p.binding (pool c p ps qs)))
                      (k.grade (withEntry (only p.binding (pool c p ps qs)) p))
-- 6.S11 [proposed, C7]: transport takes only a link from the law it carries; a link from any
-- other name carries nothing. Drawn apart, a link rarely starts at the standing's law, which is
-- the case this law tests; the laws after it take a link that does (`LinkFrom`, a field the
-- tester equates, 7.S6).
TransportNeedsTheIdentity (k : Certifying) (b : Standing) (l : Link) : Bool :=
  (k.transport b l == nothing) == not (l.from == b.law)
LinkFrom (b : Standing) : U := { l : Link | l.from == b.law }
-- 6.S11: transport gives A, on the intersection of the two reaches, with the weaker grade
TransportTakesTheWeaker (k : Certifying) (b : Standing) (l : LinkFrom b) : Bool :=
  match k.transport b l { nothing -> false ; just s -> s.law == l.to and s.grade == weakest b.grade l.standing.grade }
TransportOnTheIntersection (k : Certifying) (b : Standing) (l : LinkFrom b) (given : List (String * Value)) : Bool :=
  match k.transport b l { nothing -> false ; just s ->
    coversAll k.contains s.on given == (coversAll k.contains b.on given and coversAll k.contains l.standing.on given) }
-- 6.S13 (L10.9), for the Boolean case: transport never raises a grade nor widens a reach, and a
-- path neither (the bound is `lift true = 0` along a Boolean identity; in `C`, monotone `⊕`)
NoEvidenceForFree (k : Certifying) (b : Standing) (l : LinkFrom b) (given : List (String * Value)) : Bool :=
  match k.transport b l { nothing -> true ; just s ->
    gradeRank s.grade <= gradeRank b.grade
      and (not (coversAll k.contains s.on given) or coversAll k.contains b.on given) }
PathNeverRaises (k : Certifying) (b : Standing) (path : List (String * Standing)) : Bool :=
  match along k b path { nothing -> true ; just s -> gradeRank s.grade <= gradeRank b.grade }

-- §3. 7.S10, 7.S15: a reach as a set of values; the support holds every value
ReachesAsSets (k : Certifying) (x lo hi : Float64) (s : String) (vs : List Value) : Bool :=
  k.contains Support (Number x) and k.contains Support (Printed s)
    and k.contains (Interval lo hi) (Number x) == (lo <= x and x <= hi)
    and not (k.contains (Interval lo hi) (Printed s))
    and k.contains (Members vs) (Number x) == anyIs (Number x) vs
    and k.contains (Members vs) (Printed s) == anyIs (Printed s) vs
anyIs (v : Value) (vs : List Value) : Bool := match vs { nil -> false ; cons w rest -> w == v or anyIs v rest }
-- 12.S11: a stale entry certifies nothing, drawn as a pass of the demand's law at the highest
-- grade, covering what the demand gives, at a drawn hash
StaleCertifiesNothing (k : Certifying) (d : Demand) (e : EntryOfLaw d.law) : Bool :=
  not (k.stale d.cone (toward d e Passed (just Proved))) or not (k.serves d (toward d e Passed (just Proved)))
-- 6.S3: only a pass certifies, drawn as an entry of the demand's law at its hash, graded and
-- covering, with any outcome
OnlyAPassServes (k : Certifying) (d : Demand) (e : EntryFor d) : Bool :=
  not (k.serves d (toward d e e.outcome (just Proved))) or e.outcome == Passed
-- 7.S9, 7.S15: an entry certifies its law only under its binding, on the reach it drew, so it serves
-- a run only when that reach covers what the run gives the law: for any entry, and for a pass of
-- the demand's law at its hash with the reach it drew
CertifiesOnlyUnderItsBinding (k : Certifying) (d : Demand) (e : Entry) (f : EntryFor d) : Bool :=
  (not (k.serves d e) or (e.law == d.law and coversAll k.contains e.reach d.given))
    and (not (k.serves d (reshape f d.law f.reach Passed (just Proved))) or coversAll k.contains f.reach d.given)
-- 6.S15, D-2026-10-14-c: it serves only at or above the grade the demand accepts, drawn as a
-- covering pass of the demand's law at its hash with the grade it drew
ServesAtTheCutoff (k : Certifying) (d : Demand) (e : EntryFor d) : Bool :=
  not (k.serves d (toward d e Passed e.grade)) or (match e.grade { just g -> gradeRank d.accepts <= gradeRank g ; nothing -> false })
-- and an entry that meets all of these serves (6.S15: a certificate serves every demand it meets)
ACoveringPassServes (k : Certifying) (d : Demand) (e : EntryFor d) (g : Grade) : Bool :=
  not (gradeRank d.accepts <= gradeRank g) or k.serves d (toward d e Passed (just g))
-- 6.S5, D-2026-10-16-h (item 16): no cutoff accepts a failure, the top. Drawn as an entry of the
-- demand's law at its hash, covering and graded proved, at every cutoff `g`: a failure serves none.
-- Its control is a model whose weakest cutoff accepts a failure (tests/tester/cutoff_controls.test.lut).
NoCutoffAcceptsAFailure (k : Certifying) (d : Demand) (e : EntryFor d) (g : Grade) : Bool :=
  not (isFailure e.outcome) or not (k.serves (withAccepts d g) (toward d e e.outcome (just Proved)))
-- 6.S16: `serves` is monotone in the cutoff
ServesIsMonotoneInTheCutoff (k : Certifying) (d : Demand) (e : EntryFor d) (g : Grade) : Bool :=
  not (k.serves d (toward d e Passed e.grade) and gradeRank g <= gradeRank d.accepts)
    or k.serves (withAccepts d g) (toward d e Passed e.grade)
-- 6.S14, 12.S17: a provisional name is refused whole [proposed: its laws not listed]
ProvisionalIsRefused (k : Certifying) (es : List Entry) (n : String) (ds : List Demand) : Bool :=
  k.refuse es [{ name := n, provisional := true, laws := ds }] == [{ name := n, law := nothing, state := Provisional }]
-- 7.S15, 12.S17, 6.S3 [proposed, C5 of the round-1 review]: a disagreement at the current hash
-- (D-2026-10-15-f) refuses the law whatever serves, listed with the first the store holds, its two
-- entries named; else a failure at the current hash refutes the law (§2), so a law with one is
-- refused whatever serves, listed with the latest such failure; otherwise a law no entry serves is refused, listed with its state: the latest entry at the
-- current hash, stale when there is none there, uncertified when there is none at all. The store is
-- drawn with entries of the law at the current hash (`xs`) and at drawn hashes (`ys`) in front, so
-- that each branch is met.
RefusalSaysWhy (k : Certifying) (n : String) (d : Demand) (xs : List (EntryFor d)) (ys : List (EntryOfLaw d.law)) (es : List Entry) : Bool :=
  k.refuse (drawnStore d xs ys es) [{ name := n, provisional := false, laws := [d] }]
    == (match disputeAt k.identity d.law d.cone (drawnStore d xs ys es) {
         just (a, b) -> [{ name := n, law := just d.law, state := Disagrees a b }] ;
         nothing -> (match failureAt d.law d.cone (drawnStore d xs ys es) {
          just f -> [{ name := n, law := just d.law, state := Current f.outcome }] ;
          nothing -> if anyServes k.serves d (drawnStore d xs ys es) then nil
            else [{ name := n, law := just d.law,
                    state := (match latestAt d.law d.cone (drawnStore d xs ys es) { just e -> Current e.outcome ;
                                nothing -> (match latestOf d.law (drawnStore d xs ys es) { just f -> Stale f.cone ;
                                                                                         nothing -> Uncertified }) }) }] }) })
drawnStore (d : Demand) (xs : List (EntryFor d)) (ys : List (EntryOfLaw d.law)) (es : List Entry) : List Entry :=
  appendEntries (entriesFor d xs) (appendEntries (entriesOfLaw d.law ys) es)
-- [proposed, C5 of the round-1 review] whatever serves: a store with a failure at the current hash
-- and, in front of it, a pass made to serve the demand refuses the law, listed with the failure
-- (unless the store records a disagreement there, which is listed first, D-2026-10-15-f)
ACurrentFailureIsRefused (k : Certifying) (n : String) (d : Demand) (f : FailureFor d) (p : EntryFor d) (es : List Entry) : Bool :=
  not (disputeAt k.identity d.law d.cone (cons (toward d p Passed (just Proved)) (cons f es)) == nothing)
    or k.refuse (cons (toward d p Passed (just Proved)) (cons f es)) [{ name := n, provisional := false, laws := [d] }]
         == [{ name := n, law := just d.law, state := Current f.outcome }]
-- [D-2026-10-15-f] A replay of an entry: its identity, with an outcome and a grade drawn. When it
-- disagrees with the entry, put in front of it, the certificate has no grade whatever else it
-- holds, and the law is refused whatever serves, listed with both entries (the replay first).
replayOf (e : Entry) (o : Outcome) (g : Maybe Grade) : Entry := reshape e e.law e.reach o g
ADisagreementRefutes (k : Certifying) (c : Certificate) (e : EntryOf c) (o : Outcome) (g : Maybe Grade) (es : List Entry) : Bool :=
  agree (replayOf e o g) e or k.grade (withEntry (withEntry (prepend es c) e) (replayOf e o g)) == nothing
ADisagreementIsRefused (k : Certifying) (n : String) (d : Demand) (e : EntryFor d) (o : Outcome) (g : Maybe Grade) (es : List Entry) : Bool :=
  agree (replayOf e o g) e
    or k.refuse (cons (replayOf e o g) (cons e es)) [{ name := n, provisional := false, laws := [d] }]
         == [{ name := n, law := just d.law, state := Disagrees (replayOf e o g) e }]
-- each name and each law is refused on its own, and a cone with nothing in it is refused nothing
RefusalIsPerLaw (k : Certifying) (es : List Entry) (c : ConeName) (rest : List ConeName) (n : String) (d : Demand) (ds : List Demand) : Bool :=
  k.refuse es nil == nil
    and k.refuse es (cons c rest) == appendRefusals (k.refuse es [c]) (k.refuse es rest)
    and k.refuse es [{ name := n, provisional := false, laws := cons d ds }]
        == appendRefusals (k.refuse es [{ name := n, provisional := false, laws := [d] }])
                          (k.refuse es [{ name := n, provisional := false, laws := ds }])
    and k.refuse es [{ name := n, provisional := false, laws := nil }] == nil

Certifying := IdentityIsTheKey & StaleIsAnotherHash & NoEntryNoGrade & AFailureRefutes & OnlyCurrentEntriesCount
  & NotRunAddsNothing & WeakestAcrossBindings & StrongestUnderOneBinding & TransportNeedsTheIdentity & TransportTakesTheWeaker
  & TransportOnTheIntersection & NoEvidenceForFree & PathNeverRaises & ReachesAsSets & StaleCertifiesNothing
  & OnlyAPassServes & CertifiesOnlyUnderItsBinding & ServesAtTheCutoff & ACoveringPassServes
  & ServesIsMonotoneInTheCutoff & NoCutoffAcceptsAFailure & ProvisionalIsRefused & RefusalSaysWhy & ACurrentFailureIsRefused & RefusalIsPerLaw
  & ADisagreementRefutes & ADisagreementIsRefused

-- The store law (D-2026-10-15-f, D-2026-10-15-g; 6.S8, 6.S15, 7.S13): how an entry is written to a
-- store. A model is a way of writing one; `recordEntry` (§1) is the reference, certification_model's
-- `recording`, and certify.py's `record` its specimen. The laws are read against the latest entry
-- of the new entry's identity in the store (`known`).
Recording (k : Certifying) : { record : Entry -> List Entry -> List Entry }
-- a new identity goes in front
ANewIdentityGoesInFront (k : Certifying) (r : Recording k) (e : Entry) (es : List Entry) : Bool :=
  outOfFuel e.outcome or not (known k (k.identity e) es == nothing) or r.record e es == cons e es
-- an exact replay that agrees with the latest entry of its identity, where no disagreement stands,
-- writes nothing (6.S15: writing a certificate is idempotent)
AnAgreeingReplayAddsNothing (k : Certifying) (r : Recording k) (e : Entry) (es : List Entry) : Bool :=
  match known k (k.identity e) es { nothing -> true ;
    just f -> not (agree e f) or disputedAt k.identity (k.identity e) es or r.record e es == es }
-- one that disagrees is written beside it, in front, so the store shows the disagreement (and §3
-- refuses the law)
ADisagreeingReplayIsKept (k : Certifying) (r : Recording k) (e : Entry) (es : List Entry) : Bool :=
  outOfFuel e.outcome
    or (match known k (k.identity e) es { nothing -> true ;
          just f -> agree e f or (r.record e es == cons e es and disputedAt k.identity (k.identity e) (r.record e es)) })
-- one that agrees with the latest entry of an identity in disagreement is written too, and settles
-- it: a later agreeing pair
AnAgreeingPairSettles (k : Certifying) (r : Recording k) (e : Entry) (es : List Entry) : Bool :=
  outOfFuel e.outcome
    or (match known k (k.identity e) es { nothing -> true ;
          just f -> not (agree e f) or not (disputedAt k.identity (k.identity e) es)
                      or (r.record e es == cons e es and not (disputedAt k.identity (k.identity e) (r.record e es))) })
-- a not-run for lack of fuel is never written (D-2026-10-15-g)
AnExhaustionIsNeverStored (k : Certifying) (r : Recording k) (e : Entry) (es : List Entry) : Bool :=
  not (outOfFuel e.outcome) or r.record e es == es
Recording (k : Certifying) := ANewIdentityGoesInFront k & AnAgreeingReplayAddsNothing k & ADisagreeingReplayIsKept k
  & AnAgreeingPairSettles k & AnExhaustionIsNeverStored k

-- The law of the union (D-2026-10-16-g; L10.3, 7.S13): how many cases the union of a law's
-- stretches under a binding counts. A model is a way of counting them; `pooledCases` (§2) is the
-- reference, certification_model's `pooling`, and certify.py's `pooled` its specimen.
Pooling (k : Certifying) : { pooled : Certificate -> List Drawing -> Nat }
-- the grade of the union is monotone in the stretches: a pass whose seed (its key) the binding has
-- no stretch of adds its cases to the union and, where no failure or disagreement stands, lowers
-- no grade under its binding
TheUnionIsMonotone (k : Certifying) (p : Pooling k) (c : Certificate) (s : CurrentPass c) : Bool :=
  hasKey (stretchKey s) (stretchesUnder c.law c.cone s.binding c.entries)
    or (p.pooled (withEntry c s) s.binding == p.pooled c s.binding + stretchSize s
        and (standsOnNothing k (withEntry c s) or atMost (k.grade (only s.binding c)) (k.grade (only s.binding (withEntry c s)))))
-- a replay of a stretch (its identity again, agreeing) is not a new stretch: it adds nothing
AReplayIsNotANewStretch (k : Certifying) (p : Pooling k) (c : Certificate) (e : EntryOf c) : Bool :=
  p.pooled (withEntry (withEntry c e) (replayOf e e.outcome e.grade)) e.binding == p.pooled (withEntry c e) e.binding
Pooling (k : Certifying) := TheUnionIsMonotone k & AReplayIsNotANewStretch k

-- The law of the regions (D-2026-10-16-i, D-2026-10-16-j; 5.S8, 7.S14, L10.3): how the grade of a
-- law is read over its regions and its bindings. A model is a combination of grades, the demand's
-- choice (5.S8), and the grade it reads with it; `regionedGrade` with `orWeakest` is the
-- reference, certification_model's `regioning`, and the specimen is certify.py's grade over the
-- regions the tester records (the code that follows this draft). The regions a law is graded over
-- are given (`rs`): the type's cases, the law's `lawRegions` and the cone's branches, as the tester
-- lists them; the evidence per binding and region likewise (`ev`).
Regioning (k : Certifying) : {
  combine : Maybe Grade -> Maybe Grade -> Maybe Grade,
  graded : Certificate -> List Region -> List BindingEvidence -> Maybe Grade,
}
GradingRegion : U := { r : Region | grades r }
-- with no region the grade is the certificate's (7.S10 as §2 fixes it)
NoRegionsLeaveTheGrade (k : Certifying) (q : Regioning k) (c : Certificate) (ev : List BindingEvidence) : Bool :=
  q.graded c nil ev == k.grade c
-- a refuted certificate, or one with a disagreement, has no grade in any region
RefutedInEveryRegion (k : Certifying) (q : Regioning k) (c : Certificate) (rs : List Region) (ev : List BindingEvidence) : Bool :=
  not (standsOnNothing k c) or q.graded c rs ev == nothing
-- an author's label changes no grade, whatever cases it records under the binding of a pass
ALabelEntersNoGrade (k : Certifying) (q : Regioning k) (c : Certificate) (rs : List Region) (ev : List BindingEvidence)
                    (l : String) (n : Nat) (p : CurrentPass c) : Bool :=
  q.graded (withEntry c p) (cons { source := Label, name := l } rs) (withCases p.binding { source := Label, name := l } n ev)
    == q.graded (withEntry c p) rs ev
-- a region with no evidence under a binding leaves the law below cases (D-2026-10-16-i), whatever
-- the combination: drawn as a pass under the binding with the region's cases under it removed
belowCasesWithout (k : Certifying) (q : Regioning k) (c : Certificate) (p : Entry) (rs : List Region) (ev : List BindingEvidence)
                  (r : Region) : Bool :=
  atMost (q.graded (withEntry c p) (cons r rs) (dropCases p.binding r ev)) (just Sampled)
AnEmptyRegionLeavesBelowCases (k : Certifying) (q : Regioning k) (c : Certificate) (r : GradingRegion) (rs : List Region)
                              (ev : List BindingEvidence) (p : CurrentPass c) : Bool :=
  standsOnNothing k (withEntry c p) or belowCasesWithout k q c p rs ev r
-- and so for each of the three sources by name: the type's case, the law's side or boundary, the
-- cone's branch (D-2026-10-16-j); only a label is passed over
TheThreeSourcesGrade (k : Certifying) (q : Regioning k) (c : Certificate) (nm : String) (rs : List Region)
                     (ev : List BindingEvidence) (p : CurrentPass c) : Bool :=
  standsOnNothing k (withEntry c p)
    or (belowCasesWithout k q c p rs ev { source := FromType, name := nm }
        and belowCasesWithout k q c p rs ev { source := FromLaw, name := nm }
        and belowCasesWithout k q c p rs ev { source := FromCode, name := nm })
-- the grade over the regions is the combination, region by region, of the region's grade with the
-- grade over the rest, under one binding (the fold the reference fixes, as `pool`'s laws fix §2's)
CombinesOverRegions (k : Certifying) (q : Regioning k) (c : Certificate) (r : GradingRegion) (rs : List Region)
                    (ev : List BindingEvidence) (p : CurrentPass c) : Bool :=
  standsOnNothing k (withEntry c p)
    or q.graded (only p.binding (withEntry c p)) (cons r rs) ev
         == q.combine (regionGrade k (only p.binding (withEntry c p)) p.binding r ev) (q.graded (only p.binding (withEntry c p)) rs ev)
-- and across bindings: the combination of one binding's grade over the regions with the others'
CombinesAcrossBindings (k : Certifying) (q : Regioning k) (c : Certificate) (rs : List Region) (ev : List BindingEvidence)
                       (p : CurrentPass c) : Bool :=
  standsOnNothing k (withEntry c p)
    or q.graded (withEntry c p) rs ev
         == q.combine (q.graded (only p.binding (withEntry c p)) rs ev) (q.graded (without p.binding c) rs ev)
-- what every combination keeps, the rest being the demand's (5.S8): it is commutative, no grade is
-- its unit (a binding or a region with no grade adds nothing), and it raises no grade above the
-- stronger of the two (L10.9: no operation raises a grade without new evidence)
CombineCommutes (k : Certifying) (q : Regioning k) (g h : Maybe Grade) : Bool := q.combine g h == q.combine h g
NothingIsTheUnit (k : Certifying) (q : Regioning k) (g : Maybe Grade) : Bool := q.combine nothing g == g
CombineNeverRaises (k : Certifying) (q : Regioning k) (g h : Maybe Grade) : Bool := atMost (q.combine g h) (orStrongest g h)
Regioning (k : Certifying) := NoRegionsLeaveTheGrade k & RefutedInEveryRegion k & ALabelEntersNoGrade k
  & AnEmptyRegionLeavesBelowCases k & TheThreeSourcesGrade k & CombinesOverRegions k & CombinesAcrossBindings k & CombineCommutes k
  & NothingIsTheUnit k & CombineNeverRaises k

-- The law of the regions a law derives from its own parameters (D-2026-10-16-j, the second
-- source): a model lists them from the names of the law's cutoff parameters and of its premises;
-- `lawRegions` (§2) is the reference, certification_model's `deriving`, and the specimen is the
-- tester's reading of a law's signature. Each cutoff gives its two sides, each premise its
-- boundary, and nothing else is listed: every region is one of the law's and the count is two per
-- cutoff and one per premise, so no region is listed twice and no author's label enters.
Deriving : { ofLaw : List String -> List String -> List Region }
EachCutoffGivesTwoSides (d : Deriving) (cs ps : List String) (c : String) : Bool :=
  not (nameIn c cs) or (memberRegion (above c) (d.ofLaw cs ps) and memberRegion (below c) (d.ofLaw cs ps))
EachPremiseGivesItsBoundary (d : Deriving) (cs ps : List String) (p : String) : Bool :=
  not (nameIn p ps) or memberRegion (boundary p) (d.ofLaw cs ps)
OnlyTheLawsRegions (d : Deriving) (cs ps : List String) : Bool :=
  allIn (d.ofLaw cs ps) (lawRegions cs ps) and countRegions (d.ofLaw cs ps) == 2 * countStrings cs + countStrings ps
Deriving := EachCutoffGivesTwoSides & EachPremiseGivesItsBoundary & OnlyTheLawsRegions
